      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="walking-vs-running" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Should You Run at All?</h2>
<p>
            This is a question for your clinician rather than for us, but the equipment side of it
            is worth setting out, because the answer changes which machine to buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            If running is off the table for now, deck length stops mattering almost entirely and
            the money is better spent on cushioning quality, incline range and speed control. A
            55-inch deck is ample for walking, and that unlocks cheaper machines and easier folding.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are working back toward running, buy the 60-inch deck now. Returning to running
            on a deck that is too short forces a shortened stride at exactly the point when your
            gait needs to be as normal as possible, and that is a poor combination for a knee you
            are trying to protect. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running guide</a>
            covers deck sizing in detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            The middle path most people end up on is incline walking now, with running reintroduced
            gradually later. That argues for a machine with a strong incline range and a deck long
            enough to run on, which is where our top pick lands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. When Another Machine Is the Better Answer</h2>
<p>
            The honest version of this guide has to say when a treadmill is the wrong purchase, and
            for some readers it will be.
          </p>
          <p>
            If walking on a flat surface for twenty minutes is painful, adding a moving belt does
            not change the fundamental problem — you are still weight-bearing, and the loading is
            what hurts. A stationary or recumbent bike removes the impact entirely while allowing
            considerably more work to be done, and for genuinely irritable knees that is usually the
            better starting point. An elliptical sits between the two: weight-bearing but with no
            impact phase at all, which many people with knee pain tolerate well.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill versus elliptical comparison</a>
            works through that decision properly, including the bone-density argument that favours
            weight-bearing exercise where it can be tolerated. If a treadmill is the right machine
            but the impact is the obstacle, incline walking is the setting that resolves most of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shoes-and-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Shoes, Surface and the Things That Are Not the Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Two variables affect load through the knee as much as the deck does, and both are
            cheaper to change than the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Footwear is the first. Worn-out shoes have lost most of their midsole compression, and
            a shoe with 600 miles on it is a materially harder surface than the same shoe new.
            Replacing shoes is the least expensive intervention available and frequently the most
            effective. Whether a particular shoe type suits you is a question for a physiotherapist
            rather than a treadmill review.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second is what the treadmill stands on. A machine on thick carpet shifts under
            load, and a frame that moves slightly on each footfall introduces small instabilities
            your knee has to absorb. A firm, level base with a proper mat is worth having for
            stability reasons quite apart from noise. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills on carpet</a>
            covers why carpet causes problems for the motor as well as for stability.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="building-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. A Cautious First Six Weeks</h2>
<p>
            Equipment aside, the pattern that goes wrong is consistent: an enthusiastic first week,
            a flare-up, a fortnight off, and a conclusion that treadmills do not suit you. A slow
            build avoids it and costs nothing but patience.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weeks one and two:</strong> ten to fifteen minutes of flat walking at a speed
            that feels almost too easy, three times a week. The goal is to establish that the
            activity is tolerated at all. <strong>Weeks three and four:</strong> twenty to
            twenty-five minutes, and introduce 2 to 3% incline in one session only.
            <strong>Weeks five and six:</strong> thirty minutes, with incline in two sessions,
            building a percent at a time.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rule throughout is to change one variable at a time — duration or incline or speed,
            never two together — so that if something aggravates the knee you know what it was.
            Soreness during a session is a signal to stop that session. Soreness the following day
            that settles within a few hours is usually acceptable adaptation; soreness that persists
            into a second day means the last increase was too much.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Final Recommendations</h2>
<p>
            For most people in this category the <strong>NordicTrack Commercial 1750</strong> is
            the machine: firm cushioning with the quickest recovery we measured outside the
            commercial machines, incline to 12% for low-impact work, 0.1 mph increments, and a deck
            long enough to run on later if that becomes possible.
          </p>
          <p>
            If running is genuinely off the table and it will stay that way, the
            <strong>Sole F63</strong> does the walking job well for considerably less, with long
            side rails that are useful on a day when the knee is unhappy. If you want the best deck
            we have measured and cost is not the constraint, the <strong>Matrix T7xe</strong> is
            that machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever arrives, build slowly, change one variable at a time, and use incline rather
            than speed to make sessions harder. That last habit does more for a bad knee than any
            cushioning system on the market.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a treadmill bad for bad knees?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not inherently — a cushioned deck typically loads the knee less than pavement does. What matters is speed, incline and how gradually you build. Walking generates roughly one and a half times body weight per footfall against two and a half to three times for running, so walking with incline is usually far better tolerated than running on any machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a softer treadmill deck better for your knees?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Up to a point, then it reverses. Very soft decks absorb the elastic energy your lower leg returns on each stride, so every step costs more muscular work and you fatigue faster — and a tired, deteriorating gait is what loads a bad knee badly. Look for measurable give with a quick return to shape, not maximum softness.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is incline walking good or bad for knees?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally good, and it is the most useful setting on the machine for this purpose: it raises cardiovascular work substantially without raising impact at all. The caveat is that steep grades shift load toward the quadriceps and the front of the knee, so build from 2 to 3% a percent at a time rather than starting steep.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use the decline function if I have knee pain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Downhill walking loads the knee eccentrically — the muscles lengthen under tension to control the descent — and it is the most reliable way to aggravate an irritable knee. It is a genuinely useful function for preparing for a downhill race and the wrong function for this buyer entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What speed should I walk at with bad knees?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Whatever feels smooth and controlled, which is often between 2 and 3 mph. This is why speed increments matter: machines that adjust in 0.5 mph steps cannot express the difference between a comfortable pace and one that lengthens your stride into discomfort. Look for 0.1 mph steps and a low minimum speed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Would an elliptical or bike be better than a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Quite possibly, and it is worth considering honestly. A bike removes impact entirely; an elliptical is weight-bearing with no impact phase. If twenty minutes of flat walking is painful, a moving belt does not change the underlying loading. Weight-bearing exercise has bone-density benefits where it can be tolerated, which is the argument on the other side.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying for Knee Pain</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first of these is actively encouraged by how treadmills are marketed, which is why it is so common.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the softest deck available</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Very soft decks absorb the elastic return your lower leg needs, so each stride costs more muscular work and you fatigue faster — and a deteriorating gait late in a session is what actually loads a bad knee. Look for give with quick recovery. The deck that feels plushest in a showroom is rarely the one that feels best at minute forty.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using speed instead of incline to make sessions harder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Raising speed raises impact; raising incline does not. Incline walking increases cardiovascular work substantially at unchanged load per footfall, which is precisely the trade this buyer wants. Build the incline a percent at a time and leave the speed where it feels smooth.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the speed increment</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Machines adjusting in 0.5 mph steps cannot express the difference between a pace that feels controlled and one that lengthens your stride into discomfort, and that gap is often where the comfortable range sits. Check the increment and the minimum speed before buying — 0.1 mph steps are standard on better machines and worth insisting on.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Changing two things at once when building up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Adding duration and incline in the same week means that if the knee flares you have no idea which caused it, and the usual response is to abandon both. Change one variable at a time. Next-day soreness that settles within hours is usually fine; soreness persisting into a second day means the last increase was too much.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

